{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209992,"nonce":"HN1HVXDL/RAg1cR/fQZ0O3zrTpc=","title":"**What is wrong.** The integration tests bring up Postgres with testcontainers-go","message":"(`internal/alerts/service/main_test.go`, `internal/notification/service/main_test.go`,\n`test/integration/`). Under colima they need `DOCKER_HOST` pointing at the colima\nsocket and `TESTCONTAINERS_RYUK_DISABLED=true`; without them the container never\nstarts and the failure surfaces as a test failure deep in the suite, reading like\nbroken code rather than a missing environment variable. Neither variable is\nmentioned in `README.md`, the `Makefile`, the `justfile` or anywhere in `docs/`.\nTest runs also leave orphaned postgres containers behind, which accumulate and\neventually take the ports and the disk.\n\n**Why it matters.** `make test` is the first command a new contributor runs and\n`README.md:52` presents it as working (\"integration tests need Docker\"). Spending\nan afternoon debugging a container runtime while believing the repository is\nbroken is a bad first hour, and the far worse outcome is concluding the tests are\nflaky and skipping them — which is how a repository gets back to zero effective\ncoverage.\n\n**Where.** `README.md:48-56`, `Makefile` (`test` target), `justfile`,\n`internal/*/service/main_test.go`, `test/integration/`.\n\n**Done when.** The prerequisites are documented for colima and Docker Desktop; the\ntest harness detects a missing/unreachable Docker endpoint and fails with a\nmessage naming the variables rather than a container-start error; and containers\nare reliably reaped (ryuk enabled where it works, explicit `TerminateContainer` in\n`TestMain` where it does not) so a run leaves nothing behind.","files":null}]}